A||||7||DaRkLoRD||question regarding brakes
Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||06-26-99||02:09 PM||||I've been thinking about the braking system in the Fiero, and other cars as well. <p>first the e-brake. Would it be possible to remove the brake, and relocate it to the center console? I realise this would require a new console, and cutting a hole in the firewall as well as reroutung the cable, etc.<p>A few days ago I was reading a web page linked on here about upgrading to Beretta or Grand Am brakes, and I started wondering why there weren't any brakes with more than one set of calipers.. Assuming you used vented rotors (I've heard they're good), what about designing a system with 3 sets of calipers on each wheel? Might require a bigger master cylinder, but I think it might improve braking power.<p>what do you think?
Z||000001||lowCG||06-26-99||03:10 PM||||No reason it wouldn't work,just make sure the effort is worth the results.I've thought about this,and determined that it should work fine with just one,large caliper per wheel,12" rotors.I'm researching Toyota T-100,4x4 front calipers for possible use,they're 4 piston units.Would use Grand Am on the rear.<br>An E-brake could also be a separate deal,maybe on the final drive/axle flange,after the system is dialed in.
Z||000002||theogre||06-26-99||03:34 PM||||At first glance the might sound like an idea to add calipers, but in reallity it's not needed. I'm guessing that even with a vented rotor you couldn't get rid of the heat from it either. Fiero's brake problem generally isn't a lack of pad pressure, it's a problem of heat and waste management.<p>There are a number of options to improve Fiero brakes, and many of them are bolt on systems. Just the simple addition of good carbon metalic pads, and cross drilled rotors makes quite an improvment. It's also relativly cheap.<p>As for moving the ebrake, It's allot of work, but there's no real reason it's not posible. Seems to me tho that the loss of the console as a arm rest would be rather uncomfortable. For the amount of use that the ebrake handle gets, it seems like a heck of allot of work.
Z||000003||Eric||06-26-99||10:24 PM||||Would cross drilled rotors and carbon pads be good for the 88s as well?  I don't have a problem with the brakes right now, but a little more positive feel would be nice when I autocross.
Z||000004||theogre||06-27-99||09:37 AM||||88 uses diferant hardware than all previous years. there may be some upgrades, but don't count on it. There aren't that many 88's to make it worth the design costs for most businesses.
Z||000005||lowCG||06-27-99||09:23 PM||||Maybe some braided stainless/teflon brake lines,and some decent pads would do the trick.
Z||000006||theogre||06-27-99||10:55 PM||||They might, but I doubt you'd feel it. The number one requirement of all hydraulic hose is that it be dimensionally stable under pressure. That requirement is to insure  system pressure. Teflon can and does stretch considerably. The use of teflon is not required for DOT3-4 brake fluid. Teflon is intended for the very high heat in SI filled racing brakes. Stainless jackets on lines do not insure internal dimensional stability, the stainless prevents bursting in over pressured systems, and damage form external sources.<p>Changing the lines likely won't change performance of the system at the wheels. Fiero's brake performance problems are 2 fold, only one part of which is address in 88.<p>The first problem is heat disipation. Pre88 Fiero's solid rotors can't get rid of excecive heat of racing. Considering that the car was never intended to be raced, I don't blame GM/Pontiac for that problem at all. The second problem, waste management, is a problem on nearly every car in the U.S.<br>Cross drilling of rotors is not considered a cost effective item by the car makers. Since they aren't making race cars, This process is generally a waste of money to them.<p>again, I hate to keep beating this stick, but any changes you make to a cars brakes that eliminate things like the E brake can cause legal problems. Not only are such mods flat outlawed in many places, Insurance companies are even worse. If you are ever involved on a wreck, and there are mods to the car, Many insurance companies can make your life a real &$*@. In other words, read your policies very carefully. In many cases modifications to a car can void coverage. Having been a wrecker driver, I can tell you for sure that Insurance companies do check this stuff. The only cars these things don't apply to are trailered race only vehicles.
Z||000007||PaUL||06-28-99||01:58 PM||||One of the first upgrades I made to my car were aircraft grade braided stainless steel brake lines. I did this a long time ago, maybe 10 yrs ago. I did notice a slight improvment in brake feel, probably due to the hose not inflating slightly like the stock rubber hose would. <p>Another brake improvment I got came indirectly from the parking brake recall. Before the recall, the pads on my rear calipers had a large space between them. After the recall, the parking brakes worked and the space between the rear pads became smaller. This made the rear brakes work better or the way they were supposed to. It gripped more and activated quicker.
